# Project Development Guidelines

## Core Implementation Constraints

```tsx
// File Structure Pattern
export function DashboardFeature() {   
  // 1. Main export (named)
  // 2. Hooks strictly first
  const { data, hasError } = useQuery(...)

  // 3. arrow functions
 const name =(raw: DataSet)=> {
    return...
  }

  // 4. Subcomponents (arrow functions)
  const DataPointTooltip = ({ value }: { value: number }) => (
    <div className="bg-accent p-2 rounded-sm">
      ${value.toFixed(2)}
    </div>
  )

  // 5. Conditional enforcement
  if (hasError) {
    return <ErrorFallback className="mt-4" />
  }

  return (/* declarative JSX */)
}

// Types positioned after implementation
interface DataSegment {
  id: string
  range: [number, number]
}
```

---

## TypeScript Enforcement

- **Interfaces** over types except for unions/aliases.
- **Const objects** instead of enums:

```ts
// Good ✅
const CATEGORIES = {
  DINING: "dining",
  RETAIL: "retail",
} as const;

// Bad ❌
// enum Category { DINING, RETAIL }
```

---

## Functional Purity Rules

```tsx
// arrow function as standalone functions
function calculateMetric(transactions: Transaction[]) {
  return transactions.reduce(...)
}

// Callbacks as arrow functions
const handleUpdate = () => {
  // Closure-safe logic
}
```

